development.question_text:
The rectangular prism is twice as long as it is wide.
Which view best shows its length and width?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0); \coordinate (B) at (3,0); \coordinate (C) at (3,1.8); \coordinate (D) at (0,1.8); \coordinate (E) at (1,0.7); \coordinate (F) at (4,0.7); \coordinate (G) at (4,2.5); \coordinate (H) at (1,2.5);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--(D)--cycle; \draw (B)--(F)--(G)--(C) (D)--(H)--(G); \draw[dashed] (A)--(E)--(F) (E)--(H);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (2,4)--(2,3.2); \node[above] at (2,4) {Top};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.solution_text:
The top view shows the length and width together.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (3.2,2);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.question_text:
A triangular prism has the front view shown by its triangular end.
Which other views have rectangular outlines?
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (A) at (0,0); \coordinate (B) at (2.5,0); \coordinate (C) at (1.25,1.8);
\coordinate (D) at (1,0.7); \coordinate (E) at (3.5,0.7); \coordinate (F) at (2.25,2.5);
\draw (A)--(B)--(C)--cycle; \draw (B)--(E)--(F)--(C); \draw[dashed] (A)--(D)--(E) (D)--(F);
\draw[-{Stealth}] (1.5,-1.2)--(1.5,-0.3); \node[below] at (1.5,-1.2) {Front};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

development.solution_text:
Both the top and side views have rectangular outlines; the top view also shows the ridge line.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{scope}\draw (0,0) rectangle (3.2,2); \draw (1.6,0)--(1.6,2); \node[below] at (1.6,0) {Top};\end{scope}
\begin{scope}[shift={(4.3,0)}]\draw (0,0) rectangle (3.2,2); \node[below] at (1.6,0) {Side};\end{scope}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

